          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When one code already covers both a condition and its manifestation, is it wrong to add a second code for the manifestation?",
          "answer": "Not necessarily. It depends on the specific codes, and the retrieved passages don't settle every case.\n\nThe general rule in the ICD-10-CM conventions summary is that when a **combination code** exists, it is used **instead of** separate codes for the same conditions (coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions). So if one code already captures both the condition and its manifestation, adding a separate code for that manifestation generally goes against the convention.\n\nThe diabetes passage shows the exception. Some combination codes are still paired with an **additional code** for detail. For example, diabetic chronic kidney disease is paired with a code for the stage of kidney disease, and a diabetic foot ulcer is paired with a code for its site and depth. The combination code names the association, and the additional code adds detail (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11). Whether a second code is required or wrong therefore depends on the instructions attached to the particular code.\n\nThe passages also explain the sequencing terms \"code first\" and \"use additional code,\" which fix which code comes first, and \"code also,\" which means two codes are needed without a set order (coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions).\n\nThe retrieved material does not give a full list of which code families need a second code, so I can't confirm the answer for your specific codes. The official ICD-10-CM tabular list, with its notes at each code, is the authority to check. A certified coder can also review a specific case.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
